{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>And Moses said, etc., they cannot, etc.</b> One needs to know the substance and meaning of the matter of the difference between Moses and HaShem, for HaShem said to him, ״Go down, warn, etc.,״ and what had already been commanded earlier was not sufficient, and Moses objects and says that it is unnecessary and enough with what preceded in the way of command and warning." "And it appears that at first HaShem commanded Israel neither to ascend nor to touch the mountain in a general way, and when HaShem returned to say to Moses that the priests should be sanctified and hinted to him that they have a place on the mountain to stand upon — as we discerned from His saying ״those who draw near to HaShem shall sanctify themselves״ — He was concerned that Israel might reason with this principle, the principle of a matter that was in the general category and emerged from the general category to teach about the general category, that it emerged (from the general category), and the priests and the people and Aaron were all included in the prohibition of touching and ascending the mountain; and when the priests emerged to permit a section of the mountain, (this would) teach about the general category that it emerged, and all Israel would be permitted in the section where the priests were permitted, and even more so in the section below it, by graduated reasoning. And even though the Torah had not yet been given to know this principle, it is possible that among six hundred thousand Israelites there might be found a sage who would reason such a thing with his wisdom. Or even if no person would reason this principle on his own, nevertheless HaShem loves just judgment and could not impose the death penalty upon one who touches the mountain in the place of the priests' standing, since according to the principle of the Torah he is exempt, as mentioned. Therefore HaShem returned to say to him, ״Go down, warn the people,״ and by this He nullified (the possibility of) reasoning with this principle regarding a matter that was in the general category — for He explicitly revealed the matter to prohibit the section of the mountain where the priests would ascend. But Moses did not understand the words of HaShem and His intention as we have explained, for HaShem had not yet revealed to him the hermeneutical principles by which the Torah is interpreted, and he thought that specifically Aaron and the priests whom HaShem had permitted were permitted, but Israel remained in their prohibition. And therefore his heart cried out that this warning was unnecessary, for ״they cannot, etc.״ — they were already warned. And HaShem answered him, ״Go, descend, and you shall ascend, you, etc.,״ — and regarding the specific places that I permit to you and to Aaron and to the priests, I am warning you that you must set a boundary for them; this points with a finger that it was precisely because He had revealed the permission to them that He needed to return to prohibit them." "<b>According to</b> what we explained in His saying ״those who draw near, etc.,״ — that the intention is those who draw near to the service of the Tabernacle — the reason for the repetition of the warning will also be as we explained, on account of a matter that was in the general category; except that HaShem had not yet revealed the permission for Aaron and the priests to ascend, which itself necessitated repeating the warning a second time. And Moses immediately interjected, before the utterance of the priests' permission of ascent was completed, and said <b>they cannot, etc.</b> And HaShem returned and made him understand the implications of the matters and said to him, <b>Go, descend, and you shall ascend, you, etc., and the priests</b> — behold, He permitted a section for ascending the mountain. And immediately, right next to it, He said to him <b>and the people shall not break through</b>, meaning essentially: and the priests shall not break through to Aaron's place, and Aaron to Moses' place. And then supernal wisdom grasped, in the manner I have explained, that the concern was lest they reason regarding a matter that was in the general category, etc.; and His allusion to the Most High is in the order of the utterance, in which He prefaced by saying to Him <b>and you shall ascend, etc.</b> <b>and the priests, etc.</b>, and afterward <b>and the people shall not break through, etc.</b> And in this we find satisfaction in the second utterance that HaShem said to Moses, <b>and you shall ascend, you, etc.</b> Yet it is difficult either way: if HaShem conceded to him regarding his response, then why did He return to say a second time, ״and the people shall not break through״? This matter is straightforward, for His intention was that he should say so to Israel — if so, Moses's response was not accepted. And if we say as well that He did not concede to Moses's words, why did He not answer him immediately to refute his words, (instead of) prefacing by saying ״and you shall ascend, you and Aaron and the priests״? For HaShem should have finished the matter of the mission He had begun commanding him, of which He said to him ״warn the people, etc.,״ and afterward the El would command a new matter, namely the matter of Aaron's and the priests' ascent, which He had not mentioned until now. And with what I have explained it comes out correctly, for the command of ״warn the people״ never came except on account of the permitted boundaries of Aaron; only Moses did not wait until their completion. And therefore, in HaShem's words to him, He prefaced the completion of the matters that Moses had not waited to hear — which is the reason for the warning — and said to him <b>and you shall ascend, you and Aaron and the priests</b> — behold, He permitted a boundary of the mountain for Aaron and a boundary for the priests — and finished by saying <b>and the people shall not break through</b> to the boundaries of Aaron and the priests; rather, the entire mountain remains prohibited." "<b>Further,</b> it appears possible to say a reason for the warning a second time, namely that in the first warning no time was stated from when the prohibition begins. And although He mentioned there ״three days,״ it was not explicitly stated regarding the matter of the mountain's restriction that it begins on the third day; and so it turns out that Israel still did not know the time of its commencement. Therefore, when HaShem descended, He immediately said to Moses that he should go down to warn, etc. — that it is from this hour onward. And Moses's response requires examination. And perhaps Moses thought that the prohibition of the mountain's restriction begins from the time of their being commanded, since it was not explicitly stated ״do not touch it״ on such-and-such a day — the commencement of the mitzvah is from the time of the warning. But supernal intention was not so. And therefore the word of HaShem came strongly upon him to return to say, for this is the time of the restriction. And when we say that such was the supernal intention, that the prohibition of touching the mountain begins from the time of its command — the reason a second warning was necessary is that the first time He said ״for he shall surely be stoned, or, etc.,״ behold this is the death penalty of the court, whereas here He introduced that one whom Israel did not kill — HaShem will break forth, etc. And He was precise to use plural language, to say that even if they all break through to see, they will be judged by the judgment of Heaven and HaShem will break forth against them."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {1 "The phrase יצא מן הכלל ללמד על הכלל יצא is a tannaitic hermeneutical formula; I have rendered it unfolding its embedded wordplay rather than compressing it into a one-word label."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:30:44.451081404Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}